# What this repository claims to do, and the evidence in `make demo` for each.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The defect this codebase reliably produces is a MECHANISM WITH NO CALLER: a
# package that is built, reviewed, unit-tested and merged, and that no running
# binary can reach. Five instances by the close of Milestone 6, every one of
# them green:
#
#   #273  the piece transport      — Puller.PullPieces had no caller outside a
#                                    Go test, so the milestone it existed for
#                                    could not be demonstrated at all
#   #266  the HTTP web seed        — transfer.WebSeed is constructed nowhere
#                                    outside tests, and still is
#   #247  the fake download client — built, then never configured by the demo,
#                                    so the acceptance gate proves the by-hand
#                                    acquisition route and not the ordinary one
#   §33   partial playback         — cas.ReadPartialAt is reached only from the
#                                    peer surface; the client blob route has no
#                                    partial path, so nothing a player talks to
#                                    can serve what has not finished arriving
#   #202  HTTP/HLS                 — §33 says clients keep speaking it, and
#                                    nothing produces it
#
# Every one passed `go test -race`, `golangci-lint`, and review. Unit tests
# cannot catch this by construction: a test IS a caller, so a package with a
# thorough test suite and no production caller looks exactly like a healthy one.
#
# Static analysis cannot catch it either, and this was measured rather than
# assumed. A reachability check over exported declarations flags 318 of 1055
# (30%); restricted to funcs and methods, 149 of 618 (24%). The noise is
# structural, not a matter of tuning: a Go type returned into `:=` is never
# named by its caller, a handler reached through a registry is named only where
# it is registered, and `MarshalJSON` is called by the standard library. A guard
# that is wrong a quarter of the time is a guard people disable.
#
# What DID catch all five, every time, was one question: *what in `make demo`
# exercises this?* So that is the question this file asks mechanically.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW IT WORKS, AND WHY IT IS A LEDGER RATHER THAN A LINTER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Each claim names a fixed string that must appear in a `make demo` transcript.
# `proven` claims FAIL the build when their evidence is absent — that is the
# regression guard, and it is what stops a caller being deleted quietly.
# `pending` claims are REPORTED and do not fail: they are the known gaps, each
# carrying the issue that closes it.
#
# The two states are the same distinction `require_capability` and
# `not_exercised` already draw inside scripts/acceptance.sh (#187), for the same
# reason: an assertion whose subject is absent does not fail and does not skip.
# It quietly passes and reads as coverage.
#
# **This is a ledger and not a proof.** It catches the claims somebody wrote
# down, exactly as `make hygiene` catches the name shapes somebody thought of.
# Its value is that adding a mechanism now has a place where its absence is
# visible, and that flipping `pending` to `proven` is a deliberate act with a
# string attached to it.
#
# The evidence is a FIXED STRING, matched literally, never a regex. A regex here
# would drift into matching a sentence that merely mentions the feature — and
# the failure mode this file exists for is precisely a claim that reads as
# coverage without being it.
#
# 🔴 EVIDENCE MUST BE ASSERTION TEXT, NEVER NARRATION. The demo prints an
# epilogue describing what the run proved, and that epilogue prints whether or
# not the section it describes ran. This file's first version used `took the
# PIECE path` for the cooperative-acquisition claim, and the sabotage run that
# deleted the entire swarm section did not catch it — the phrase survived in the
# epilogue's own prose. Two of that section's three claims failed and the third
# passed against a paragraph of narration.
#
# So: take the string from an `assert_*` description or from a log line the code
# itself emits. If a phrase would still be printed by a run that did nothing,
# it is not evidence.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   claim <id>
#   state proven|pending
#   ref   <spec sections, ADRs, issues>
#   why   <one line: what would be untrue if this stopped happening>
#   issue <number>            (pending only: what closes it)
#   evidence <fixed string that must appear in the demo transcript>
#
# A stanza ends at the next `claim` or at end of file.

claim cooperative-acquisition
state proven
ref   §23, §24, ADR-0041, ADR-0042, #267
why   Two peers assemble one blob from pieces rather than one pulling it whole from the other. This is the branch Milestone 6 added, and it went unproven for most of that milestone because the transport had no caller.
evidence the branch this milestone added is the one that ran

claim cooperation-is-a-number
state proven
ref   §23, #267
why   The external source served strictly less than two full copies. Without this the peers converge without cooperating, which is the shape M6 replaced and which passes every other assertion.
evidence THE COOPERATION CLAIM

claim clients-never-learn-what-a-piece-is
state proven
ref   §33, §85, #267
why   A blob that arrived as pieces from several machines range-reads over ordinary HTTP and causes no piece anywhere. This is what keeps piece exchange an internal optimisation rather than a client requirement, and it is an assertion about an ABSENCE, which is the easiest kind to lose.
evidence AND THE PIECE RECORD DID NOT MOVE

claim bytes-cross-the-peer-surface
state proven
ref   §21, §32, ADR-0030, ADR-0012
why   The byte-carrying hop is the mTLS peer surface and the controller carries nothing. Asserted from the SENDING side, because a destination's account of what it fetched is a claim about itself.
evidence served these bytes on its peer surface

claim placement-converging
state proven
ref   §56, §64, M4-11
why   A real gap in real bytes on a second peer is reported as converging and names which peer is missing them. The milestone Milestone 4 exists for.
evidence PLACEMENT_CONVERGING: one of two required peers holds the bytes

claim gc-refuses-what-it-cannot-establish
state proven
ref   §19, §53, ADR-0018, M4-12
why   The collector will not unlink the last local copy unless it can affirmatively establish the bytes survive elsewhere, and records peer_unreachable as a reason rather than an error. Under ADR-0038 a peer will routinely be unable to establish it, so this refusal is load-bearing rather than an edge case.
evidence THE LAST COPY IS STILL THERE

claim lazy-chunking-by-a-job
state proven
ref   §16, ADR-0034, M5
why   Ingest chunks nothing and no sweep chunks anything; a manifest is a job's product and never a lookup's. A sweep that quietly started chunking everything would still be green everywhere else.
evidence and no chunk_blob job exists at all — not even one waiting to run

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PENDING — mechanisms that exist and that nothing running reaches.
#
# Each of these is a package with tests and no path from the binary. They are
# listed rather than left implicit so that the gap is visible at the gate
# instead of being rediscovered by whoever writes the next acceptance section.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

claim web-seed-is-a-source
state proven
ref   §27, ADR-0013, ADR-0042, #266
why   A member that does not speak piece exchange is driven as a web seed — its ordinary content route carries part of a swarm transfer and it serves no piece at all. Before #266 nothing in the tree could produce such a member: Blobs and Pieces were built from one store in one call, so transfer.WebSeed was constructed nowhere outside a Go test.
evidence AND IT SERVED NO PIECES AT ALL

claim device-authenticates-as-user
state proven
ref   §40, ADR-0048, ADR-0032, #303
why   A device authenticates as its user over the real middleware chain, presenting only its key and a user-signed enrolment cert — no token is issued. This is M8's premise, and it went unreachable through M8-02: the enrolment primitives, the control-plane store and the Device scheme were each built and unit-tested with no path from the binary that enrolled a user, enrolled a device, and then authenticated as it. The negatives ride the same section — no credential is a 401, a revoked device is a 401 — so a green run cannot mean "the endpoint answered" rather than "it authenticated someone".
evidence a device authenticates as its user with only a cert and a possession proof — no token issued

claim polled-acquisition-completes
state proven
ref   §58, §61, §65, ADR-0025, #247
why   A release is handed to a download client that takes it, and something asks the client what it finished. Before #247 nothing enqueued poll_downloads, so a transfer was never asked about again and no arc could pass QUEUED on a running node — the gate proved the by-hand adoption route and nothing else.
evidence a grab_release job succeeded

claim playback-over-a-partial-blob
state proven
ref   §33, §84, ADR-0043, ADR-0044, #29
why   cas.ReadPartialAt serves bytes out of a blob still being assembled and was reached only from the peer surface. M10 adds the client route's partial path (internal/api/blobs, ADR-0044): it consults the availability record, serves a landed range over ordinary HTTP, and BLOCKS on a range that has not landed rather than serving the hole. Before this nothing a player talks to could serve what had not finished arriving — Milestone 10's premise, and the fourth mechanism-with-no-caller this ledger names. The negative rides alongside: a range in the hole times out rather than being handed zeroes, so a green run cannot mean "it served something" when what it served was a hole.
evidence a client range-read a blob that had not finished arriving

claim time-critical-piece-priority
state proven
ref   §33, §84, ADR-0043, #29
why   The local peer prioritises the pieces near the playback window (§33), so a player's next bytes arrive next rather than whenever the swarm converges on them. The reader publishes where it is stalled (a byte offset), the store carries it across the role boundary invariant 4 allows, and the driver converts it to a piece index and fetches that window before falling back to rarest-first. Without a demo the mechanism is invisible: the driver would adopt a playhead nothing wrote, or write one nothing reads — the same mechanism-with-no-caller shape M6 hit. Here a node is given a playback window before it joins the swarm and its transfer logs that it prioritised the piece at that offset, proving the whole chain ran.
evidence the transfer fetched the playback window first

claim clients-are-served-hls
state pending
issue 202
ref   §33, §70
why   §33 opens by saying clients continue to consume standard HTTP/HLS, and nothing in the tree produces a playlist. Milestone 10 is built on that sentence.
evidence an HLS playlist was served

claim control-plane-backup-and-restore
state proven
ref   §49, §50, §51, §82, ADR-0044
why   Milestone 7. A peer's own control plane and its Ed25519 identity are the two things nothing else in the fabric can give back. M7-02 (#282) delivers the backup — a VACUUM INTO snapshot with signed provenance — and a restore that opens it (unit-proven: state at the moment, refused as a control plane, tampered backup refused). The demo takes one against the running controller and proves the generation reports real change: a re-take with no change is the SAME generation, and a control-plane write advances it. The push that carries it to a peer is control-plane-backup-reaches-peers; the rebuild that reads it back is recover-from-peer-is-recognised.
evidence a control-plane change advances the backup generation

claim control-plane-backup-reaches-peers
state proven
ref   §50, ADR-0046, #283
why   A control-plane backup is not kept only where it was taken: a peer takes a signed snapshot of its own control plane and PUSHES it to every Full Peer that trusts it, and the receiver verifies and holds it. Nothing pulls a control plane, so unless it was pushed across before the disk was lost there is nothing to rebuild from — this is the half that makes recover-from-peer-is-recognised possible. The receiver's own account of the generation it holds is the fact, because a sender's belief about what a peer holds is a claim about itself (the durability lesson ADR-0046 is written around).
evidence the control-plane backup crossed to the peer that trusts this one

claim recover-from-peer-is-recognised
state proven
ref   §51, §82, ADR-0044, ADR-0046, #284
why   The milestone's sentence. A peer that loses its disk is rebuilt from a peer that trusted it, comes back with the SAME identity, and no other peer is re-enrolled or reconfigured. The evidence is the third clause, and it is asserted FROM THE OTHER PEER: the rebuilt node dials the survivor over mTLS and the survivor names it as the same peer, from the key it pinned before the loss — nobody reconfigured. A restore that completes but leaves the fabric no longer trusting the node passes every assertion about the restore itself and is exactly the failure this catches; "the restore applied" is deliberately NOT the evidence.
evidence the peer that trusts it recognised the rebuilt node as the same peer, with no re-enrolment

claim pairing-enrols-a-new-device
state proven
ref   §40, ADR-0022, ADR-0038, #305
why   An OLD device authorises a NEW one over a dumb relay: the two exchange public keys and a salt, derive the same short code, and on a human match the old device signs an enrolment cert the new device stores — the server trusted with nothing (ADR-0038). Before this the pairing primitives (the SAS, the commitment) and the relay each existed with no path from the binary that ran an actual handshake: internal/pairflow and internal/pairrelay were built and unit-tested, and no command reached them. This is that caller, end to end, over the real HTTP relay.
evidence the new device is enrolled by the old one over the relay — paired, no server trusted

claim pairing-substitution-changes-the-code
state proven
ref   §40, ADR-0022, #305
why   The short authentication string binds BOTH keys, so a man-in-the-middle that substitutes the responder key derives a DIFFERENT code, which the two humans catch. This is the whole security argument for a 7-digit code, and it is the property commit-before-reveal (internal/pairing/commitment.go) makes true against a rushing attacker; measured here by deriving the code over an honest key and over a substitute and asserting they differ.
evidence a substituted responder key yields a DIFFERENT short code — the MITM the humans catch

claim recovery-reconstructs-the-identity-offline
state proven
ref   §79, ADR-0022, #306
why   Key loss is total data loss (ADR-0021), so a recovery secret is minted once at generate and the identity is derived deterministically from it — the secret ALONE reconstructs the same identity, same public key peers pinned, on a machine with no surviving device and no server. Before this the recovery primitive (internal/recovery.DeriveUserSeed) derived a seed that nothing generated FROM or recovered TO; useridentity.Generate drew a raw-random key that no secret could regenerate. Now generate derives from the secret and `identity recover` rebuilds from it offline. A recover that produced a different key would pass any "it worked" check and fail every pinned peer, so the evidence is the public key, not an exit code.
evidence recovery reconstructs the SAME identity from the secret alone, offline — the public key peers already pinned

claim recovered-device-authenticates
state proven
ref   §79, §40, ADR-0022, ADR-0048, #306
why   Recovery is not done when the key is back: the recovered identity must be usable. The recovered machine enrols its own device under the reconstructed identity offline, and that device then authenticates as the same user over the real middleware chain, presenting only its cert — no token issued. This closes the arc from "the secret reconstructs a key" to "a person is back in", and rides an auth-enabled node because the Device scheme is only reached where a credential is required.
evidence the recovered device authenticates as the same user — offline recovery, then a live request, no token issued

claim wrong-recovery-secret-fails-loud
state proven
ref   §79, ADR-0022, #306
why   A mistyped secret must be refused by its checksum rather than reconstructing a different, plausible-but-wrong identity in silence (ADR-0022's SLIP-39 concern, applied to the base secret). recovery.ParseSecret rejects a corrupted bech32m string, and the `identity recover` wiring surfaces that loudly instead of deriving garbage. Asserted by flipping one character in the checksum tail and confirming the command refuses before any key is derived.
evidence a corrupted recovery secret is refused loudly, never turned into a wrong identity

claim personal-state-stored-as-ciphertext
state proven
ref   §38, §42, §41, ADR-0049, #320
why   Invariant 6 is the whole point of the personal-state plane: the server stores personal state it cannot read. A device mints a space key, wraps it for the authorised devices, and pushes an encrypted change; the peer stores the opaque space, the wrapped keys and the ciphertext, holding no key to unwrap any of it. The demo decodes the peer's stored change bytes and confirms the plaintext item is nowhere in them, the authorised devices decrypt it client-side, and a device the space was never wrapped for cannot. Without this the plane would be a plaintext store with an encryption-shaped API.
evidence the peer stores the change as ciphertext — the plaintext item is nowhere in the bytes at rest

claim pairing-binds-the-encryption-key
state proven
ref   §41, ADR-0049, ADR-0022, #336
why   The pairing SAS must bind each device's X25519 ENCRYPTION key, not only its signing key: without it a relay present during pairing could keep the honest signing key yet substitute the device's encryption key, getting itself enrolled as a wrap target for the user's spaces (§41) before the cert is signed. The v2 commit-reveal flow now commits to and reveals both keys, and the demo shows that swapping only the encryption key yields a different short code — the wrap-target MITM the humans catch — mirroring the signing-key substitution.
evidence a substituted responder ENCRYPTION key yields a DIFFERENT short code — the wrap-target swap the humans catch too

claim personal-state-replicated-as-ciphertext
state proven
ref   §37, §45, ADR-0038, ADR-0049, #323
why   Encrypted personal state replicates to every trusted Full Peer by default (§37, §45): it is small and less replaceable than media, so a device syncs against whichever site it can reach. Without this a space created on one peer would be readable only there, and losing that peer would lose the state. The demo replicates a space from peer A to peer B and shows an authorised device reading it from B — B holds the whole encrypted space (metadata, wrapped keys, changes) as ciphertext it cannot itself read, and only the device's key opens it.
evidence the second peer holds the encrypted space and an authorised device reads it there — replicated as ciphertext

claim personal-state-converges-after-partition
state proven
ref   §42, §43, ADR-0038, ADR-0049, #324
why   The personal-state plane is deliberate multi-master (§43): unlike the control plane it stays writable on both peers during a partition and merges on reconnect, with the semantic merge CLIENT-SIDE (§42). This is the milestone's payoff and the second evidence line epic #28 owes. The demo partitions two peers, has a device write on each, reconnects and replicates both ways, and asserts the two devices materialise the SAME state (order-independent, all three concurrent items) — while peer B's stored bytes stayed ciphertext throughout, so the server merged nothing and read nothing. A server-side merge, or an order-dependent one, would break it.
evidence the two devices converge to the same state after an offline concurrent edit, merged client-side

claim personal-state-snapshot-bounds-the-log
state proven
ref   §44, ADR-0049, #325
why   Without snapshots and compaction a long-lived space is an unbounded change log every new or long-offline device must replay — the sync a phone cannot afford (#330). A snapshot is a materialised CRDT state at a causal point, encrypted under the space key; compaction drops the changes the snapshot subsumes and every replica holds. The demo takes a snapshot, adds a tail change, compacts away the three pre-snapshot changes, and shows a device STILL reaching the full four-item state from the snapshot plus the tail — while the snapshot at rest is ciphertext the server cannot read. Compaction is bounded by the acknowledged frontier so a partitioned peer's change is never dropped (a store test).
evidence a device reaches the full state from the snapshot plus the tail, after the pre-snapshot changes were compacted away

claim personal-state-revocation-cuts-access
state proven
ref   §41, ADR-0022, ADR-0049, #361
why   Revocation is only real if the revoked device actually loses access. A rotation mints a fresh space key, re-wraps it for the remaining recipients, DELETES the revoked device's stored copy, then snapshots the current state under the new key and compacts the old log the snapshot subsumes — so the revoked device holds no copy of any key it can open and can read nothing encrypted from here on. The demo wraps a space for three devices, revokes one, and asserts the revoked device can no longer read (its wrapped key is gone) while a remaining device still reads the state from the post-rotation snapshot, and the peer holds a wrapped key for the two remaining devices only. A rotation that left the revoked copy in place, or re-keyed for it too, would let the revoked read succeed; a rotation that broke the remaining devices would fail their read. Forward-looking, not retroactive: it keeps what it already decrypted (ADR-0022's honesty).
evidence a revoked device can no longer read the space — its wrapped key is gone and the space was re-keyed without it

claim subsonic-client-browses-and-plays
state proven
ref   §70, §33, ADR-0013, #30
why   The external plane's first adapter: an existing music client talks to Heyarr over OpenSubsonic, browses the server-readable catalogue and plays from it — Heyarr owning no client. The signature defect this whole file exists to catch is a mechanism with no caller, and a protocol-conformant handler no real app drives is exactly that; so the demo drives the adapter the way a real Subsonic app would — authenticate with a bearer token as the password, derive an artist index and album list from the ingested music, open an album, and STREAM a track. The stream is proven honest by resolving the song's edition through the ordinary assets API and fetching its blob through the ordinary blob route, then asserting the two byte streams are identical: the adapter delegates byte serving (ADR-0013) rather than inventing it, and rides a real caller on real bytes. A conformant handler serving fabricated or empty bytes would fail the byte-identity assertion.
evidence the bytes a Subsonic client streams are byte-identical to the blob route's

claim subsonic-serves-no-personal-state
state proven
ref   §72, §73, #30, #372
why   Invariant 6 is load-bearing here: playlists, play-counts, scrobbles and starred items are personal state, encrypted and opaque to the controller (§72), so a controller-side adapter cannot serve them faithfully and must not fabricate them. The adapter is scoped to the server-readable library + stream; personal-state list types (starred/recent/frequent) return an empty list rather than a fabricated ranking, and the playlist methods are not served at all. Exposing that state is the deliberate device-side Personal MCP second step (§73, #372), not a controller feature. An adapter that quietly returned an empty-but-successful playlist, or a fabricated "most played", would read as coverage while breaking the invariant — so the demo asserts the empty starred list and the refused playlist method explicitly.
evidence a personal-state album list (starred) is empty, not fabricated

claim subsonic-real-app-in-the-loop
state pending
issue 373
ref   §70, #30
why   make demo proves protocol conformance and a real byte path, but not the honest far end of the bar: a real Subsonic APP (Symfonium/DSub/Feishin) browsing and playing. That is app-in-the-loop and out of the demo budget, exactly as #202 keeps the real video client out of M2's demo. Recorded pending, not skipped.
evidence a real Subsonic app browsed and played

claim opds-reader-browses-and-downloads
state proven
ref   §69, §70, ADR-0013, #30
why   The publications half of the external plane: an existing OPDS reader talks to Heyarr over OPDS 1.2, browses the server-readable catalogue and downloads from it — Heyarr owning no client. Like the OpenSubsonic adapter this risks the mechanism-with-no-caller defect this file exists to catch: a protocol-conformant feed no real reader drives. So the demo drives it the reader's own way — a Basic challenge, the navigation feed, the acquisition feed — then downloads a publication and proves the bytes are honest by resolving the acquisition link's edition through the ordinary assets API, fetching its blob through the ordinary blob route, and asserting the two byte streams are identical: the adapter delegates byte serving (ADR-0013) rather than inventing it. A feed that offered a link to fabricated or absent bytes would fail the byte-identity assertion.
evidence the bytes an OPDS reader downloads are byte-identical to the blob route's

claim opds-real-reader-in-the-loop
state pending
issue 376
ref   §69, §70, #30
why   make demo proves protocol conformance and a real byte path, but not the honest far end of the bar: a real OPDS reader APP (KOReader/Foliate/Marvin) browsing and downloading. That is app-in-the-loop and out of the demo budget, exactly as #202 keeps the real video client out of M2's demo and #373 the Subsonic app. Recorded pending, not skipped.
evidence a real OPDS reader browsed and downloaded

claim acquires-over-plain-http
state proven
ref   §58, #30
why   §58's "additional acquisition clients" includes plain HTTP, and it is the one where HEYARR is the client rather than driving an external daemon — so it is the one provable end to end without a mock. The demo stands up a real HTTP server on a real port serving deterministic bytes, offers a release whose source is that URL, and drives the ordinary acquisition arc: the grab tries the plain-HTTP client first, it takes the http source and REFUSES a magnet (so the magnet want beside it still falls through to the fake — the clients compose, registry.Grab), fetches over a real connection into the path-map dir, and the bytes that land are asserted byte-identical to what the server served. The mechanism-with-no-caller trap is defeated the same way the OpenSubsonic and OPDS scenes defeat it: a real caller (the grab) drives a real fetch, proven by digest rather than by a passing conformance handler.
evidence the bytes Heyarr fetched over plain HTTP are byte-identical to what the server served

claim acquires-over-daemon-clients
state pending
issue 379
ref   §58, ADR-0026, #30
why   Proving a real DAEMON transfer (a torrent actually downloaded by qBittorrent, an .nzb by SABnzbd/NZBGet) is kept out of the demo BY DESIGN, not by a missing harness: ADR-0026 decided a download client is an operator-managed service Heyarr never installs or runs in CI, so its transfer is proven by an opt-in, env-gated live test against a real instance (TestLiveQBittorrent), skipped when unset. The client itself is a real configured caller (see qbittorrent-is-a-configured-download-client); what stays pending is only the end-to-end daemon transfer, honest by the repo's own standard.
evidence a real qBittorrent transfer completed

claim dlna-device-browses-and-plays
state proven
ref   §70, #202, #30
why   The video-and-audio client story: the devices that matter (a television, a speaker) speak DLNA natively, so they browse a UPnP ContentDirectory and play — Heyarr owning no client. The mechanism-with-no-caller trap here is a ContentDirectory that answers Browse but hands out res URLs nothing can fetch; so the demo drives it as a real device would — a SOAP Browse of the root, then a folder, then a fetch of the item's res URL — and asserts those bytes are byte-identical to what the ordinary blob route serves. The res URL is a render capability (ADR-0040), so the device fetches with no credential and the byte path is the proven one, not reimplemented. A conformant Browse handler emitting decorative or wrong res URLs would fail the digest.
evidence the bytes a DLNA device fetches from a res URL are byte-identical to the blob route's

claim dlna-real-device-plays
state pending
issue 382
ref   §70, #202, #30
why   make demo proves the ContentDirectory contract — a control point Browses and fetches — but not SSDP LAN advertisement (a UDP-multicast responder, untestable headlessly) or a real Samsung TV / Devialet discovering, browsing and playing (app-in-the-loop). Both are deferred to #382, the #202/#373 pattern. Recorded pending, not skipped.
evidence a real Samsung TV browsed and played
